ed on the design for managing Cron jobs: They\u0027ll be project-universal and attributed to a specific bot ID, and will trigger isolated execution runs. The next hurdle is how they\u0027ll handle context. When a job runs, it will execute in a clean, ephemeral runner that does not load the entire chat history. Instead, the runner will load the task payload and relevant workspace files. A receipt card will be posted in the original thread indicating job completion.\n\n\n**Evaluating Bot Communication**\n\nI\u0027m now focusing on bot communication styles within a thread. I\u0027m considering two methods: adopting the Slack-style @mentions or introducing an active persona switcher within the input dock. For Tier 1 and 2 (Team Room/Task Workstation), the conversation history is shared, but each bot maintains its own identity and private tools. In Tier 4 (Private Scratchpad), you converse one-on-one with the selected bot. I\u0027m also considering long chat thread context management, inspired by AVOS and its in-flight `compact_context` for milestone summarization.\n\n\n**Defining Context Retention**\n\nI\u0027ve been refining how to manage context, specifically in relation to background Cron jobs and long chat threads. I\u0027ve concluded that Cron jobs maintain pristine, small context and utilize a \"Receipt Card\" pattern to avoid thread bloat. I\u0027m now focusing on ensuring that any automatic AST/checkpoint squash summarizes milestone completions into the Project Journal and trims older turns, only retaining the summary and the most recent three turns.\n\n\n",
