      "text": "Your instinct here is sharp: **tying autonomous cron executions directly to the chat history of a long thread is a major architectural trap.** \n\nIf a 50-message chat thread is used to run a scheduled cron:\n1. **Context Overflow \u0026 Token Waste:** Loading 50 messages into the LLM context just to run a simple 3:00 AM check burns 40,000 tokens per wake-up.\n2. **Conversation Hijacking:** If you are actively talking to `@ReviewBot`, and `@DevBot` suddenly wakes up and dumps 3 pages of compiler logs into the middle of your screen, the thread becomes unreadable.\n\nHere is the exact architectural blueprint to solve **in-chat bot switching**, **decoupled crons**, and **thread context bloat** cleanly.\n\n---\n\n# 1. The Core Rule: Crons are Project-Universal, Bot-Attributed, and Thread-Decoupled\n\n```\n┌────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐\n│                                   THE CRON EXECUTION LIFECYCLE                                         │\n├────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤\n│ 1. SCHEDULED IN THREAD: User or Bot says: \"Check dependency vulnerabilities every Monday at 9 AM\"     │\n│                         Recorded in SQLite as a Project-Universal Cron with `ownerBotId: \"dev-bot\"`.   │\n├────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤\n│ 2. WAKES IN ISOLATION:  Cron fires via Cloudflare Workflow in a **pristine context (\u003c 2k tokens)**.    │\n│                         It does NOT load the 50-message chat history of the thread!                    │\n├────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤\n│ 3. ASYNC RECEIPT CARD:  Posts a concise 2-line **Receipt Card** to the thread (or Team Room).         │\n│                         Zero conversation hijacking. You click to expand logs only if you want.        │\n└────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘\n```\n\n---\n\n# 2. Where Do Crons Live and Execute?\n\nCrons are **Project-Universal entities attributed to a specific functional Bot ID**:\n* They are registered in the project\u0027s central table: `project_scheduled_jobs` (in `SpaceDO` SQLite).\n* They store: `id`, `projectId`, `ownerBotId`, `originThreadId`, `cronExpression`, and `taskPayload`.\n\n```\n                                  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────┐\n                                  │      `SpaceDO` PROJECT CRON REGISTRY         │\n                                  │   Cron: \"0 9 * * 1\" | Owner: @DevBot         │\n                                  │   Origin Thread: #102 | Payload: { ... }     │\n                                  └──────────────────────┬───────────────────────┘\n                                                         │\n                                                         ▼ (Fires at 09:00 UTC)\n┌────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐\n│ TIER 3 CLOUDFLARE WORKFLOW / CLEAN RUNNER (Pristine Context \u003c 2,000 Tokens)                            │\n├────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤\n│ • Loads `@DevBot`\u0027s `SOUL.md` + the exact `taskPayload` + relevant files from `/workspace`.            │\n│ • **Zero chat history loaded.** No token bloat. No prompt pollution.                                   │\n│ • Runs `npm audit` / AST checks in Tier 2 Isolate or Tier 6 Container.                                 │\n│ • If changes are made: Commits a Git branch `cron-audit-v1` and opens a PR.                            │\n└────────────────────────────────────────────────┬───────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘\n                                                 │\n                                                 ▼ (Emits WebSocket Event via WsHubDO)\n┌────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐\n│ 📬 INLINE BACKGROUND JOB RECEIPT CARD (Rendered in Origin Thread or Team Room)                         │\n│ ┌────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐ │\n│ │ ⏰ **Background Cron Run Completed** (by **@DevBot**)                              [ 09:01 UTC ]   │ │\n│ │ Task: *Weekly Dependency Security Audit*                                                           │ │\n│ │ Status: 🟢 **All 42 packages secure. 0 vulnerabilities found.**                                     │ │\n│ │ [ 🔍 View Full Output Logs ]                               [ 🌿 Inspect Commit `c_8a12` ]          │ │\n│ └────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘ │\n└────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘\n```\n\n### Why this solves your scenario:\n* **If you switched to `@ReviewBot` in that chat:** You don\u0027t get interrupted. You and `@ReviewBot` continue chatting smoothly. The cron result appears as a **compact notification card** from `@DevBot` in the timeline, indicating that a background job ran.\n* **If the bot is not selected:** It still runs. A cron belongs to the *project capability*, not your active typing cursor.\n\n---\n\n# 3. Solving Thread Bloat: \"What if the thread is too big?\"\n\nWhen human-agent threads run for days, they accumulate thousands of tokens. AVOS prevents thread decay through a **Two-Pronged Defense**:\n\n```\n┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐\n│                   THE THREAD CONTEXT BLOAT DEFENSE                          │\n├─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤\n│ 1. CRON CONTEXT DECOUPLING (Invisible Slicing)                              │\n│    Background jobs NEVER inherit chat messages. They execute with a         │\n│    clean slate containing only the task spec and target workspace files.    │\n├─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤\n│ 2. IN-FLIGHT MILESTONE SQUASHING (`compact_context`)                        │\n│    When a chat thread crosses 25 turns (or 30,000 tokens):                  │\n│    • The agent writes completed decisions to `PROJECT.md` / Project Journal.│\n│    • The thread history is squashed: old turns are archived to D1.          │\n│    • The active prompt window keeps only: `PROJECT.md` summary + last 4 turns│\n│    • Result: The thread runs forever at a constant, fast latency!           │\n└─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘\n```\n\n---\n\n# 4. Switching Bots Within the Same Chat Thread\n\nYou don\u0027t need confusing bot rosters or multiple parallel chat boxes. You handle bot switching inside any thread using **Two Intuitive Controls**:\n\n```\n┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐\n│ 💬 THREAD #102: Stripe Billing Integration                                [ 🌿 branch:task_102 ] [ ⚙️ ]│\n├─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤\n│ 👩‍💻 **Alice:** \"We need to verify if the webhook handles duplicate charge events.\"                     │\n│                                                                                                         │\n│ 🤖 **@DevBot:** \"I\u0027ve added an idempotency key check in `src/api/stripe.ts` (Commit `c_3f91`).\"         │\n│                                                                                                         │\n│ 👩‍💻 **Alice:** \"@ReviewBot, please audit @DevBot\u0027s idempotency patch for edge cases.\"                   │\n│                                                                                                         │\n│ 🤖 **@ReviewBot:** \"Auditing `src/api/stripe.ts` diff:                                                  │\n│    - Idempotency table insertion is atomic.                                                             │\n│    - Verified: duplicate webhook payloads return 200 OK without double-crediting. Approved.\"           │\n├─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤\n│ 📝 COMPOSER DOCK:                                                                                       │\n│ [ 🤖 Active Operator: @ReviewBot ▼ ]  [ 🔒 Personal Credentials: Off ]                                  │\n│ ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐ │\n│ │ Type a message, or use @DevBot, @ReviewBot to direct specific tasks...                              │ │\n│ └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘ │\n└─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘\n```\n\n### How Switching Operates Under the Hood:\n1. **The `@mention` Fast Route (Slack Style):**  \n   If you type `@DevBot`, the router activates `@DevBot`\u0027s instructions and toolset for that turn. If you type `@ReviewBot`, it uses `@ReviewBot`\u0027s reviewer guidelines.\n2. **The Dropdown Selector (Active Default Operator):**  \n   In the bottom composer, a pill shows: `[ 🤖 Active: @DevBot ▼ ]`. Clicking it lets you switch the default responder for subsequent messages without typing `@` every time.\n3. **Message Attribution in SQLite:**  \n   In `SpaceDO`, every message is stamped with `sender_id: \"usr_alice\"` or `sender_id: \"agent_dev_bot\"`. When any bot reads the conversation history, it knows exactly who said what.\n\n---\n\n# 5. Database Schema: Managing Universal Crons in `SpaceDO` SQLite\n\n```sql\n-- Project-Universal Scheduled Jobs Table (Sp