ning Declarative Workflows**\n\nI\u0027m now focusing on how to enable workflow customization. Instead of asking developers to write code, I\u0027m exploring declarative JSON pipelines. These pipelines would define steps like context enrichment using MCP tools (e.g., `find_impact`), agent execution, and verification. The agent could inherently know how to use MCP tools based on attached gadgets, simplifying customization and reducing code complexity. Security is still a priority, with Gatekeepers acting as safeguards.\n\n\n**Refining Gadget Security**\n\nI\u0027m now zeroing in on the security benefits of the Gadget-centric approach. Running Gadgets in V8 isolates, managed by Gatekeepers, is incredibly powerful. This means no unchecked access to the host filesystem, no direct exposure of API keys, and no arbitrary shell execution. This, along with developers being able to build Gadgets quickly using Preact/Hono templates, or even through AI prompts, simplifies the entire architecture. We\u0027ve avoided the previous pitfalls of a bloated framework with this focus on background indexers as autonomous gadgets.\n\n\n",
