      "text": "**Defining Project Architecture**\n\nI\u0027m currently focused on AVOS\u0027s architecture, specifically how projects act as central containers. I see projects encompassing all workflow classes, including Company Automations. This centralized structure is key for managing and organizing our various processes.\n\n\n**Organizing Automations in Projects**\n\nI\u0027ve refined my thinking to integrate \"Company Automations\" directly within projects. This means each automation, like `StripeReconciler`, would reside within a project such as \"Finance \u0026 Treasury.\" This organization streamlines workflow management, associating each automation with a project, Git repository, and agents for maintenance. Public projects will create a system where all users and assistants are aware of the running company automation.\n\n\n**Addressing Assistant Collisions**\n\nNow, I\u0027m grappling with the potential for assistant collisions within shared projects. The core challenge is preventing duplicate work when multiple assistants, like Alice\u0027s and Bob\u0027s, detect and attempt to fix errors in the same company automation or task. I\u0027m exploring solutions to coordinate their actions, ensuring they collaborate effectively, and prevent wasted credits on redundant fixes or conflicting pull requests.\n\n\n**Analyzing Collaboration Challenges**\n\nMy current focus is on how to facilitate collaboration within a shared company space. The challenge is ensuring assistants, even mini-AGIs, can claim and coordinate work without collisions, duplicated effort, or RBAC violations. I\u0027m exploring distributed systems and multi-agent systems to solve this. I\u0027m specifically looking at a project-bound automation architecture and a distributed work claiming protocol for a lease/ownership approach.\n\n\n**Implementing Task Lease System**\n\nI\u0027m now diving into the specifics of a task claiming mechanism. I\u0027ve designed an atomic SQLite CAS write using a `project_tasks` table to implement a lease system. For example, if Alice\u0027s assistant sees an error, it attempts an atomic write to update the task status to `IN_PROGRESS` and claim the lease. If successful, Alice proceeds with the fix; if not, Bob\u0027s assistant knows Alice is working on it and subscribes to PR updates, preventing duplicate work. This system should be resilient.\n\n\n**Refining Assistant Collaboration**\n\nI\u0027m now thinking about \"Collaborative Witness\" mode where assistants can observe and contribute. Bob\u0027s assistant might now inform him about Alice\u0027s work on an incident, linking to her PR. I\u0027m also looking into semantic deduplication before execution. A `task.find_or_create` system is being considered to prevent duplicate work, using vector search or DAG checks to identify and address similar tasks, ensuring efficient collaboration.\n\n\n**Enforcing Security Protocols**\n\nI\u0027m now implementing robust security measures. I\u0027m focusing on role and permission enforcement within projects. My assistant can only claim tasks based on my assigned `project_members` permission level, which is determined by my user role. Junior developers will be unable to modify sensitive financial tasks because the system will prevent it. This will prevent unauthorized changes. Also, the system will prevent redundant requests.\n\n\n",
