      "text": "This naming convention is much cleaner:\n* **Templates** \u003d The turnkey, pre-configured whole-space blueprints (e.g., *\"LexPulse Legal Cowork\"*, *\"CodePilot Software Studio\"*).\n* **Extensions** \u003d The atomic, modular add-ons that plug into a project (e.g., the *Tree-Sitter Code Indexer Extension*, the *Stripe Invoicing Extension*).\n\n---\n\n### Part 1: How Plandex-Style Project Indexers Work\n\nTools like Plandex handle massive repositories (millions of tokens) by avoiding a naive \"dump the whole codebase into the prompt\" approach. Instead, they use a **Hierarchical Code Map (Project Graph)**:\n\n1. **AST Parsing via Tree-Sitter:** It runs a lightweight parser (`web-tree-sitter`) that breaks files down into Abstract Syntax Trees, extracting *only* the symbols (function names, class signatures, method declarations, exports, and imports), stripping away internal function bodies and whitespace.\n2. **Structural Hierarchical Indexing:** It builds a compressed tree outline of the entire repository (e.g., File A defines `class User` which imports `verifyToken` from File B).\n3. **Targeted Symbol Retrieval:** When an agent is given a massive task (e.g., *\"Refactor authentication across 40 files\"*), it queries the indexer: *\"Where is `verifyToken` used?\"* The indexer returns the exact 3 files and 15 lines that matter, keeping the AI\u0027s context window under 4,000 tokens instead of 2,000,000 tokens.\n\n---\n\n### Part 2: Implementing a Plandex-Style Indexer as an AVOS \"Extension\"\n\nIn AVOS, you don\u0027t need a heavy terminal daemon for this. You package this exact capability into an **Extension** that combines a **Dynamic Worker (Tier 2)**, a **Dynamic Workflow (Tier 3)**, and a **DO Facet SQLite Database (Micro-App)**.\n\n```\n┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐\n│                       AVOS EXTENSION: `ext_tree_sitter_indexer`             │\n├─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤\n│ 1. DYNAMIC WORKFLOW (Tier 3)         │ Watches file changes in `/workspace` │\n│    (`indexer-workflow.ts`)           │ and batches code for parsing.        │\n├──────────────────────────────────────┼──────────────────────────────────────┤\n│ 2. DYNAMIC WORKER / V8 ISOLATE       │ Runs `web-tree-sitter` (Wasm) in     │\n│    (Tree-Sitter Engine)              │ sub-milliseconds to extract ASTs.    │\n├──────────────────────────────────────┼──────────────────────────────────────┤\n│ 3. DO FACET SQLITE DATABASE          │ Stores the relational symbol graph   │\n│    (Extension Persistence)           │ (`symbols`, `imports`, `call_edges`).│\n├──────────────────────────────────────┼──────────────────────────────────────┤\n│ 4. AUTO-GENERATED MCP v2 ENDPOINT    │ Exposes tools like                   │\n│    (`/mcp`)                          │ `ext_indexer_find_references` to AI. │\n└──────────────────────────────────────┴──────────────────────────────────────┘\n```\n\n---\n\n### Part 3: Concrete Implementation of the Code Indexer Extension\n\n#### 1. The Extension Manifest (`extension.json`)\n```json\n{\n  \"id\": \"ext_tree_sitter_code_indexer\",\n  \"name\": \"Plandex-Style Tree-Sitter Code Indexer\",\n  \"version\": \"1.0.0\",\n  \"description\": \"Indexes massive repositories using WebAssembly Tree-Sitter, building a relational symbol graph for precise AI navigation.\",\n  \"runtime\": {\n    \"engine\": \"dynamic_worker_isolate\",\n    \"database\": \"do_facet_sqlite\",\n    \"enableMcpV2Api\": true\n  },\n  \"backgroundWorkflows\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"reindex_workspace\",\n      \"scriptPath\": \"./workflows/reindex.ts\",\n      \"trigger\": \"file_modified_batch\"\n    }\n  ]\n}\n```\n\n#### 2. The Background Workflow (`workflows/reindex.ts`)\nWhen files change in the project workspace, Cloudflare Workflows kicks off an incremental background re-index:\n```typescript\nimport { WorkflowStep, WorkflowEvent } from \"cloudflare:workers\";\n\nexport async function execute(event: WorkflowEvent\u003c{ changedFiles: string[] }\u003e, step: WorkflowStep, ctx: any) {\n  const { changedFiles } \u003d event.payload;\n\n  // Step 1: Parse changed files using Tree-Sitter in a V8 isolate\n  const symbolGraphs \u003d await step.do(\"parse-ast-batch\", async () \u003d\u003e {\n    const results \u003d [];\n    for (const filePath of changedFiles) {\n      const sourceCode \u003d await ctx.fs.read(filePath);\n      // Run WebAssembly Tree-Sitter parser\n      const symbols \u003d await ctx.treeSitter.parseAst(filePath, sourceCode);\n      results.push({ filePath, symbols });\n    }\n    return results;\n  });\n\n  // Step 2: Commit relational symbol updates to the Extension\u0027s private DO SQLite DB\n  await step.do(\"update-symbol-sqlite\", async () \u003d\u003e {\n    for (const item of symbolGraphs) {\n      ctx.storage.sql.exec(\"DELETE FROM code_symbols WHERE file_path \u003d ?\", item.filePath);\n      for (const sym of item.symbols) {\n        ctx.storage.sql.exec(\n          \"INSERT INTO code_symbols (id, file_path, symbol_name, symbol_kind, signature) VALUES (?, ?, ?, ?, ?)\",\n          `${item.filePath}:${sym.name}`, item.filePath, sym.name, sym.kind, sym.signature\n        );\n      }\n    }\n  });\n\n  return { indexedFilesCount: changedFiles.length };\n}\n```\n\n#### 3. How the Agent Uses the Indexer Extension via MCP\nBecause the extension automatically exposes an MCP v2 `/mcp` endpoint, the universal agent doesn\u0027t need custom prompt instructions. It simply calls the extension\u0027s tools during a large task:\n```typescript\n// The agent queries the extension\u0027s symbol graph instead of reading 50 raw files\nconst symbols \u003d await ctx.mcp.call(\"ext_tree_sitter_code_indexer\", \"ext_indexer_find_references\", {\n  symbolName: \"verifyToken\"\n});\n```\n\n---\n\n### Part 4: Why This Architecture Wins\n\n1. **Modular Extensions:** Developers can build extensions for anything (a Code Indexer, a Financial Ledger Grapher, a Legal Citation Mapper) and publish them to the marketplace.\n2. **Zero Pollution:** The indexer runs as a background micro-app. It doesn\u0027t clutter the user\u0027s source code repository with hidden config folders.\n3. **Massive Context Savings:** Instead of feeding 2M tokens of raw code into an LLM and paying hundreds of dollars in inference costs, the agent queries the Extension\u0027s DO SQLite symbol graph and pulls **only the exact 3,000 tokens** required to solve the task.",
