      "text": "Here is the comprehensive, production-grade architectural specification of all **130 finalized features and system plans**. \n\nThis document contains **no code snippets**—instead, every item provides the complete **mental model, operational mechanics, system invariants, and failure-mode defenses** so that any AI receiving this list can implement the entire operating system in strict, dependency-aware order.\n\n---\n\n# THE AVOS MASTER IMPLEMENTATION BLUEPRINT (PHASES 1–8)\n\n```\n┌────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐\n│                                   CHRONOLOGICAL IMPLEMENTATION PHASES                                  │\n├─────────┬────────────────────────────────────────────┬─────────────────────────────────────────────────┤\n│ Phase 1 │ Multi-Tenant Core \u0026 Storage Foundation     │ Durable Objects, SQLite FS, Git Mesh, Subspaces │\n├─────────┼────────────────────────────────────────────┼─────────────────────────────────────────────────┤\n│ Phase 2 │ Compute Ladder \u0026 Runtime Hypervisor        │ 6-Tier Execution, FUSE Sync, Containers, Wasm   │\n├─────────┼────────────────────────────────────────────┼─────────────────────────────────────────────────┤\n│ Phase 3 │ Action Space, Virtual CLI \u0026 Decoupled Auth │ `ctx` SDK, JIT CLIs, Outbound Workers, Vault    │\n├─────────┼────────────────────────────────────────────┼─────────────────────────────────────────────────┤\n│ Phase 4 │ Cognition, Memory, Knowledge \u0026 Ingestion   │ Dual-Tier DAGs, Vectorize ACLs, Parse 5, Search │\n├─────────┼────────────────────────────────────────────┼─────────────────────────────────────────────────┤\n│ Phase 5 │ 4-Tier Threads \u0026 Multi-Assistant Governance│ Team/Task/Scratchpad Threads, Leases, RFC Gates │\n├─────────┼────────────────────────────────────────────┼─────────────────────────────────────────────────┤\n│ Phase 6 │ Project Kinds, Viewports, Daemons \u0026 Gadgets│ 7 Studio Kinds, Tree-Sitter Indexers, DO Facets │\n├─────────┼────────────────────────────────────────────┼─────────────────────────────────────────────────┤\n│ Phase 7 │ Asymmetric Workflows, Queues \u0026 Telephony   │ Inbound Webhooks, Queues, Replay Lake, Voice    │\n├─────────┼────────────────────────────────────────────┼─────────────────────────────────────────────────┤\n│ Phase 8 │ Billing, Marketplace, Distribution \u0026 Shell │ Credits, Subscriptions, FDE Ladder, Radar, UI   │\n└─────────┴────────────────────────────────────────────┴─────────────────────────────────────────────────┘\n```\n\n---\n\n## PHASE 1: Multi-Tenant Core, Storage \u0026 Workspace Foundation\n\n#### 1. Enterprise Depth Hierarchy\n* **Mental Model:** A two-level organizational tree modeling corporate structures. Depth 0 represents the Parent Company Space (Holding Co / Root) holding master billing, corporate compliance rules, and global brand identities. Depth 1 represents Department Sub-Spaces (Legal, Engineering, Support) operating with specialized studio viewports and localized token spend caps.\n* **Underlying Mechanics:** Stored in Cloudflare D1 with composite indexing over tenant, parent space, and depth. All spaces share uniform database schemas; differences between holding companies and departments are governed entirely by depth parameters and capability manifests.\n* **Invariants:** Depth cannot exceed 1 (strictly avoiding recursive tree bloat). Deleting a parent space cascades soft-deletions to all departmental sub-spaces.\n\n#### 2. Sub-Space Inheritance \u0026 Isolation Matrix\n* **Mental Model:** Downward oversight coupled with strict upward and lateral air-gapping. Parent space owners/admins automatically inherit administrative visibility across all departmental sub-spaces. Department members cannot view parent company balance sheets or executive rooms. Sibling sub-spaces have zero visibility into each other by default.\n* **Underlying Mechanics:** Evaluated by edge middleware on every incoming request. Checks target space hierarchy; if parent membership holds owner/admin rank, downward access is granted. Lateral calls between sibling spaces return 403 Forbidden unless accompanied by an active cross-space capability grant.\n* **Invariants:** Fail-closed security posture. If hierarchy lookup fails or experiences transient latency, the middleware defaults to access denial.\n\n#### 3. Public vs. Private Project Access Control\n* **Mental Model:** Projects within any space are categorized as Public Commons (visible to all verified space members) or Confidential Workstations (locked strictly to explicitly invited members).\n* **Underlying Mechanics:** Managed by single-threaded SQLite inside `SpaceDO`. Private projects maintain an internal membership table with role rankings. Non-members attempting to read files, list threads, or inspect private project metadata receive a 404 Not Found response.\n* **Invariants:** Private projects must never return 403 Forbidden, ensuring their topical existence is completely hidden from unauthorized employees.\n\n#### 4. Project-Anchored Automation Architecture\n* **Mental Model:** Automations, background pipelines, and cron routines never float unattached at the root space level. Every company automation is anchored inside a dedicated, version-controlled project container (e.g. Treasury Automations live in `Project: Treasury`).\n* **Underlying Mechanics:** Every automation inherits the project’s Git repository, testing harness, and membership access rules. The Space Assistant acts as an executive overseer that reviews status but delegates code modifications to the project\u0027s specialized operators.\n* **Invariants:** Deleting or archiving a project automatically pauses and archives all bundled background automations.\n\n#### 5. Cloudflare Artifacts Git-for-Agents Engine\n* **Mental Model:** A native, serverless Git engine running directly inside Durable Objects via a Zig-to-WASM compilation layer. Gives every project a real, full-fidelity Git repository for commits, branches, and diffs.\n* **Underlying Mechanics:** Exposes bindings to programmatically initialize repositories, generate 10ms zero-copy branch forks, compare commit tree hashes, and execute atomic fast-forward merges.\n* **Invariants:** All codebase modifications must be committed as valid Git commit trees with SHA-256 parent hashes; loose unversioned string overwrites are prohibited.\n\n#### 6. In-Memory Virtual POSIX Shell (`@cloudflare/computer`)\n* **Mental Model:** A sub-millisecond in-memory shell that executes 90%+ of agent terminal actions (`grep`, `cat`, `sed`, `awk`, `find`, `jq`, `diff`, Unix pipes) directly in V8 without starting a Docker container.\n* **Underlying Mechanics:** Uses `just-bash` to parse POSIX syntax in memory, operating directly against the authoritative Durable Object SQLite virtual filesystem.\n* **Invariants:** Never escalates to a container for text, line-inspection, or file-search operations. Returns results in under 5ms for fractions of a cent.\n\n#### 7. Clean Namespace Separation (`/workspace/` vs. `/.avos/`)\n* **Mental Model:** Strict physical boundary between user application code and internal agent operating system meta-state.\n* **Underlying Mechanics:** `/workspace/` contains application source code tracked by Git Artifacts. `/.avos/` is Git-ignored and contains agent state, shared skills, background workflow code, and private scratchpad drafts.\n* **Invariants:** The frontend file tree hides `/.avos/` by default. Git commits strictly ignore all files outside of `/workspace/`.\n\n---\n\n## PHASE 2: Compute Ladder \u0026 Invisible Runtime Hypervisor\n\n#### 8. 6-Tier Cloudflare Execution Ladder\n* **Mental Model:** A hierarchical compute ladder spanning from zero-cost in-memory virtual filesystems up to full containerized operating systems, routing tasks dynamically based on required system capability.\n* **Underlying Mechanics:** Tiers: 1 (DO SQLite VFS), 2 (V8 Isolate Shell), 3 (Durable Workflows), 4 (Kitesurf Browser), 5 (Headless Chromium), 6 (Linux Container Sandbox).\n* **Invariants:** Workloads always start in the lowest capable tier and promote upward only upon encountering missing binary or runtime requirements.\n\n#### 9. Invisible Runtime Hypervisor\n* **Mental Model:** The AI model is never burdened with managing 6 compute tiers in its prompt. It interacts via 3 universal execution primitives (`Bash`, `Read`, `Edit`) and 2 sensory primitives (`Browser`, `Delegate`). The runtime hypervisor inspects commands and routes them to the appropriate tier invisibly.\n* **Underlying Mechanics:** The hypervisor inspects terminal commands under `Bash`. POSIX commands stay in V8; browser commands route to Kitesurf; native compilation commands route to containers.\n* **Invariants:** The LLM context window never contains tier-selection instructions, eliminating prompt paralysis.\n\n#### 10. `ArtifactFS` FUSE Driver with Lazy Hydration\n* **Mental Model:** Linux containers mount project workspaces on the fly without waiting for a full `git clone` or downloading hundreds of megabytes of cold files.\n* **Underlying Mechanics:** Uses a FUSE filesystem driver inside the container to fetch file contents on-demand across Cloudflare\u0027s edge network, shaving 90 seconds off container boot times.\n* **Invariants:** Writes made inside the container flush back to the authoritative DO SQLite filesystem upon `fsync`.\n\n#### 11. 4-Dimension State Synchronization Engine\n* **Mental Model:** Guarantees zero split-brain when switching between fast V8 Isolates and heavy Linux Containers.\n* **Underlying Mechanics:** Synchronizes state across 4 vectors: Filesystem (ArtifactFS FUSE), Shell Session (`cwd` and `export` variables), Git State (shared commit HEAD), and Database State (in-memory DO Facet SQLite).\n* **Invariants:** A working directory change in an isolate immediately applies when a container launches.\n\n#### 12. 95/5 Compute Arbitrage with 60-Second Micro-Sleep\n* **Mental Model:** Delivers unconstrained compute performance while keeping monthly infrastructure bills under $2.50 per user.\n* **Underlying Mechanics:** 95% of tasks execute in sub-millisecond V8 Isolates. Heavy containers launch only for native compilation and shut down after a strict 60-second idle countdown.\n* **Invariants:** Containers must never remain warm indefinitely. Alarms terminate idle containers automatically.\n\n#### 13. In-Isolate WebAssembly Python (Pyodide)\n* **Mental Model:** Standard Python scripts (math, json, data analysis) execute in V8 isolates without container boot overhead.\n* **Underlying Mechanics:** Embeds Pyodide (Wasm CPython) inside Tier 2. Automatically promotes to Tier 6 Containers only when scripts import native C-extensions like PyTorch or OpenCV.\n* **Invariants:** Pyodide execution incurs zero container compute charges.\n\n#### 14. Tier 4 Cloudflare Kitesurf Agent Browser\n* **Mental Model:** A 30MB low-memory browser engine built specifically for agent web navigation, running 3–7x faster and cheaper than Chromium.\n* **Underlying Mechanics:** Handles DOM extraction, article parsing, and form navigation in milliseconds. Automatically elevates to Tier 5 Headless Chromium if the page requires WebGL or visual screenshot inspection.\n* **Invariants:** Kitesurf is always attempted first for web navigation unless visual QA is explicitly requested.\n\n#### 15. Virtual Display Desktop Automation (\"Computer Use\")\n* **Mental Model:** Enables agents to interact visually with real desktop software (LibreOffice, Blender, desktop apps) using simulated mouse and keyboard inputs.\n* **Underlying Mechanics:** Runs Xvfb (virtual display server), Fluxbox, and `xdotool` inside Tier 6 Containers. Streams live visual frames over WebSockets via noVNC to an interactive canvas.\n* **Invariants:** Actions operate within sandboxed userland with no host network escape capabilities.\n\n#### 16. Multiplexed Persistent gRPC Bridge\n* **Mental Model:** Replaces high-latency HTTP JSON serialization between Durable Objects and Linux Containers with binary streams.\n* **Underlying Mechanics:** Establishes persistent binary socket pooling over Cloudflare TCP sockets (`connect(socket)`), streaming compiler logs and AST diffs with sub-5ms latency.\n* **Invariants:** Sockets reconnect automatically upon container micro-sleep recycles.\n\n#### 17. Instant Zero-Copy Branch Micro-Forking\n* **Mental Model:** Subagents and private scratchpads receive instant, isolated sandboxes without cloning physical disk storage.\n* **Underlying Mechanics:** Leverages Cloudflare Artifacts copy-on-write pointers to fork repositories in under 10ms.\n* **Invariants:** Mutations on forks have zero effect on the main branch until merged through a Pull Request.\n\n#### 18. Turn-by-Turn Git Time Machine\n* **Mental Model:** Total auditability and instant recovery. If an agent introduces a bug, the user can scrub backward and restore any historical point in time.\n* **Underlying Mechanics:** Every completed tool turn automatically generates an immutable Git commit in Cloudflare Artifacts, enabling 1-click historical rollbacks.\n* **Invariants:** Resetting to an earlier turn preserves branching history without data loss.\n\n#### 19. Reasoning-Aware Git Blame\n* **Mental Model:** Git history records not just *what* code changed, but the exact architectural reasoning *why* it changed.\n* **Underlying Mechanics:** Every automated commit embeds structured front-matter metadata in the commit message containing author agent role, user goal, legal/technical rationale, and task ID.\n* **Invariants:** Inspecting git blame exposes the decision chain that justified the line of code.\n\n---\n\n## PHASE 3: Action Space, Virtual CLI \u0026 Decoupled Auth\n\n#### 20. Universal Runtime SDK (`ctx`)\n* **Mental Model:** A standardized, strongly typed runtime environment injected into every isolate, workflow, and container execution.\n* **Underlying Mechanics:** Exposes scoped namespaces: `ctx.fs` (files), `ctx.project` (tasks/journals), `ctx.space` (workspace admin), `ctx.vault` (auth), `ctx.cli` (terminal), and `ctx.connectors` (third-party tools).\n* **Invariants:** Injected methods are bound server-side to the caller\u0027s verified identity and role rank.\n\n#### 21. Universal Vault Layer \u0026 Edge Outbound Workers\n* **Mental Model:** Zero-leak credential security. Code written by users or agents never holds raw API keys or database passwords.\n* **Underlying Mechanics:** Secrets are encrypted with AES-256-GCM in D1/KMS. Outbound Workers intercept HTTPS traffic and inject decrypted authorization headers at the edge network boundary.\n* **Invariants:** Plaintext secrets never land on container disks, isolate memory, or client logs.\n\n#### 22. WriteGuard Mutation Interceptor\n* **Mental Model:** An unbypassable edge firewall preventing prompt-injected agents from deleting production databases or running destructive shell commands.\n* **Underlying Mechanics:** Parses SQL ASTs and tool parameters at the edge proxy layer, blocking destructive operations (`DROP`, `DELETE *`, `rm -rf`) unless authorized by a signed Gatekeeper token.\n* **Invariants:** Unauthenticated or untrusted callers cannot execute destructive mutations under any circumstance.\n\n#### 23. Virtual CLI Interception Engine\n* **Mental Model:** External CLI commands (`stripe`, `gh`, `r2`, `jq`) run in milliseconds without launching a container or installing binaries.\n* **Underlying Mechanics:** Intercepts terminal commands inside V8 Isolates and maps them to edge REST API fetches or in-memory WebAssembly executions.\n* **Invariants:** Returns terminal-formatted ASCII tables to the agent in under 5ms for $0.0001.\n\n#### 24. Just-In-Time (JIT) Virtual CLI Synthesis\n* **Mental Model:** The agent writes its own CLI wrappers on the fly as it encounters new third-party APIs, eliminating the need to pre-bundle hundreds of SDKs.\n* **Underlying Mechanics:** When an unregistered command is called, the agent synthesizes a 15-line parser using `ctx.cli.register`, saves it into SQLite, and executes it in V8 forever.\n* **Invariants:** Only the specific subcommands requested are implemented, keeping code footprints tiny.\n\n#### 25. Decoupled Logical Connector Binding Matrix\n* **Mental Model:** Source code never contains hardcoded account handles or email addresses. Scripts reference abstract aliases that users map dynamically.\n* **Underlying Mechanics:** Code calls `ctx.connectors.use(\"inbound_mail\")`. A relational binding table in `SpaceDO` maps that alias to an active physical connection (Company Gmail vs. Personal Gmail).\n* **Invariants:** Switching connected accounts in the UI re-binds the alias without modifying application code.\n\n#### 26. Pre-Flight Scope Verification \u0026 Incremental Re-Auth\n* **Mental Model:** Prevents silent failures when an automation needs higher permissions than originally granted.\n* **Underlying Mechanics:** The SDK validates granted OAuth scopes before calling an action. If write permissions are missing on a read-only token, it halts and surfaces an incremental upgrade card.\n* **Invariants:** Automations never fail with cryptic 403 errors; they cleanly prompt for the missing permission.\n\n#### 27. Gatekeeper Capability-Based Cross-Subspace Delegation\n* **Mental Model:** Fine-grained, unforgeable capability tokens that allow cross-department collaboration without broad database access.\n* **Underlying Mechanics:** Mints HMAC-SHA256 tokens specifying allowed operations, target resource IDs, and expiration timestamps.\n* **Invariants:** An agent can never mint a capability token exceeding the permission ceiling of the human user who authorized it.\n\n#### 28. 3-Tier Connector Scoping\n* **Mental Model:** Strict separation between company-wide, project-bound, and personal integrations.\n* **Underlying Mechanics:** Connectors are scoped as `Space-Shared` (company Stripe), `Project-Bound` (clinic WhatsApp line), or `User-Personal` (Alice\u0027s private Gmail).\n* **Invariants:** Unattended system crons cannot access personal credentials without an active user session.\n\n#### 29. Public Channel Tool Stripping \u0026 MCP-UI Privacy Bridge\n* **Mental Model:** Protects personal user accounts from accidental leakage in public team channels.\n* **Underlying Mechanics:** In public rooms, personal tools are physically stripped from the LLM prompt. If a user asks for personal data, the agent emits an interactive card to fork to a private scratchpad.\n* **Invariants:** Personal credentials can never be executed or queried in shared public rooms.\n\n#### 30. Single-Use Ephemeral Action Grants\n* **Mental Model:** Allows quick one-off personal actions in public rooms without leaving the channel.\n* **Underlying Mechanics:** Uses MCP v2 Elicitation to display an authorization card visible only to the owning user. Approving mints a single-use token valid for 15 seconds.\n* **Invariants:** The token expires immediately upon tool execution; other team members never see the credential.\n\n#### 31. Turn-Holder vs. Referent Mention Router\n* **Mental Model:** Prevents multiple bots from waking up simultaneously when one is merely being talked about.\n* **Underlying Mechanics:** The leading `@mention` is assigned the primary turn lock. Subsequent mentions (e.g. `@DevBot\u0027s patch`) are parsed as contextual references and do not wake up.\n* **Invariants:** Only one agent holds the active execution turn lock in a thread at any given moment.\n\n#### 32. Sequential Turn Delegation\n* **Mental Model:** Orderly handoffs between agents without human babysitting.\n* **Underlying Mechanics:** An active agent calls `ctx.agent.delegate()` to pass structured tasks to another specialist agent sequentially, transferring the execution turn lock cleanly.\n* **Invariants:** Simultaneous multi-agent responses in the same thread are disallowed.\n\n#### 33. Cryptographic Delegation Chaining\n* **Mental Model:** Prevents privilege escalation through delegation (Trojan Delegation attacks).\n* **Underlying Mechanics:** Tasks carry an HMAC-signed token with the initiating human user\u0027s ID and role rank. Project agents evaluate the human\u0027s clearance, not the assistant\u0027s.\n* **Invariants:** A junior user cannot trick a project agent into editing administrative files via delegation.\n\n#### 34. Demonstration-to-Skill Distillation Engine\n* **Mental Model:** \"Watch me do it once, automate forever.\" Turns manual workflows into permanent, zero-cost TypeScript code.\n* **Underlying Mechanics:** Captures execution traces, parameterizes dynamic arguments, verifies exit code 0 in a V8 sandbox, and saves the script to `/.avos/shared/skills/`.\n* **Invariants:** Subsequent invocations execute deterministically in 5ms without re-prompting an LLM.\n\n#### 35. Multimodal Video-to-Skill Learning Engine\n* **Mental Model:** Allows non-technical operators to train agents by uploading 60-second screen recordings of complex workflows.\n* **Underlying Mechanics:** Transcribes audio via Workers AI Whisper; vision models extract clicks and inputs, compiling them into parameterized TypeScript.\n* **Invariants:** Synthesized code is verified in a Kitesurf sandbox before registration.\n\n#### 36. Zero-Turn Tool Catalog in Prompt\n* **Mental Model:** Eliminates token-wasting directory exploration loops (`ls /skills`).\n* **Underlying Mechanics:** Dynamically compiles a compact index of available commands into the system prompt. Implementation code is fetched only when executed.\n* **Invariants:** The agent knows all available capabilities on Turn 1 without issuing discovery commands.\n\n#### 37. Declarative Step Pipelines (Execution Hooks)\n* **Mental Model:** Allows users and templates to customize agent workflows safely without writing dangerous execution loops.\n* **Underlying Mechanics:** Configured via JSON: defines pre-flight context hooks (querying background indexers) and post-turn verification gates (`npm test`).\n* **Invariants:** Custom steps are declarative and execute within sandboxed platform harnesses.\n\n#### 38. Egress Data Loss Prevention (PII Scrubber)\n* **Mental Model:** Prevents sensitive company information from leaking to public social or messaging channels.\n* **Underlying Mechanics:** The outbound proxy scans payloads sent to consumer connectors (X/Twitter, Discord, Instagram) for credit cards, SSNs, and private keys.\n* **Invariants:** Payloads containing unmasked sensitive data are blocked and flagged for review.\n\n---\n\n## PHASE 4: Cognition, Memory, Knowledge \u0026 Ingestion\n\n#### 39. Universal Agent Execution Kernel (`NaelaoAgentDO`)\n* **Mental Model:** A single, rock-solid execution kernel powering all agent roles across the platform.\n* **Underlying Mechanics:** Implements the REPL loop, token optimization, memory recall, and tool dispatching inside an isolated Durable Object.\n* **Invariants:** Specialized roles differ only by configuration and capability firewalls, not separate codebases.\n\n#### 40. Dynamic 4-Layer System Prompt Assembly\n* **Mental Model:** Assembles system prompts dynamically from 4 immutable layers on every turn to prevent prompt drift.\n* **Underlying Mechanics:** Combines Universal Kernel Rules $\\rightarrow$ Business Context $\\rightarrow$ Agent `SOUL.md` $\\rightarrow$ Founder Directives.\n* **Invariants:** User custom overrides cannot override universal operating directives or capability firewalls.\n\n#### 41. Hardware-Enforced Role Firewalls (`AgentFirewall`)\n* **Mental Model:** Guarantees that customer-facing or junior bots can never execute shell commands or edit files.\n* **Underlying Mechanics:** Strips disallowed tool schemas from the prompt at the API gateway layer based on declared role.\n* **Invariants:** A support bot physically receives zero execution tool definitions in its context window.\n\n#### 42. Dual-Tier 