      "text": "**Considering Calendar Unification**\n\nI\u0027m currently dissecting the user\u0027s statement about a unified calendar. It seems they view it as a connector offering shared space for various calendar choices, like Google Calendar. This contrasts with my previous understanding, which involved a `SpaceDO.space_calendar_events` aspect. I\u0027m trying to reconcile these perspectives and grasp the user\u0027s intent.\n\n\n**Refining Calendar Architecture**\n\nI\u0027m now fully immersed in the implications of treating the calendar as a Space-Shared MCP Connector. The key takeaway is avoiding a siloed, hardcoded calendar within the AI system. This means businesses can seamlessly integrate their existing systems, like Google or Outlook, through the MCP framework.  The `checkAvailability()` and `bookSlot()` functions become universally applicable across various calendar providers. This is a big step in the right direction!\n\n\n**Clarifying Connector Implementation**\n\nI\u0027ve made significant headway aligning with the user\u0027s focus on logical aliases and avoiding hardcoded providers. The concept of a Standardized Logical MCP Connector Category, specifically \"calendar,\" is taking shape. It supports various providers, including Google, Outlook, CalDAV, and even a local SQLite fallback, with a default `SPACE_SHARED` scope. The associated MCP v2 tools, like `check_availability` and `book_slot`, will handle the core calendar interactions.\n\n\n**Defining Calendar Functionality**\n\nI\u0027m now zeroing in on the mechanics of the calendar integration. It seems that when a user, like Dr. Smith, has a Google Workspace calendar linked, an event automatically generates in Google Calendar, complete with invitations and a Google Meet link. In the Outlook case, the Microsoft Graph API is leveraged for event creation. For unmapped scenarios, a fallback is implemented.\n\nI\u0027m tackling multi-calendar conflict checks and timezone normalization, critical invariants. I\u0027m also planning for atomic slot booking to handle race conditions that might arise with external calendars.\n\n\n**Addressing Calendar Concurrency**\n\nI\u0027m now focusing on concurrency issues, specifically the potential for double-booking. I\u0027m exploring temporary edge mutexes to prevent overlaps, using a lock with a Time To Live (TTL) before releasing it upon successful API booking. Additionally, I\u0027m working on implementing webhooks to manage calendar changes from external sources, ensuring the system remains synchronized with updates from the likes of Google Calendar. This will be the key to our goal of keeping AVOS in sync with client changes on the calendar.\n\n\n",
